  "paper": "paper_2",
  "question": {
    "html": "<section class=\"context\"><p>A rod, R, lies perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field B of strength 0.50 T directed into the plane of the page. R is connected to a circuit and the electric current I<sub>R</sub> is 2.0 A.</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"a.i\"><p><strong>a.i</strong> Calculate the magnitude of the force per unit length on R. [2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"a.ii\"><p><strong>a.ii</strong> </p><p>Identify on the diagram the direction of the force on R.</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure></p><p>[1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b\"><p><strong>b</strong> </p><p>B is removed and a wire Z, carrying a current I<sub>Z</sub>, is placed parallel to R.</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_02\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 2\" src=\"asset:fig_02\"/></figure></p><p></p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b.i\"><p><strong>b.i</strong> </p><p>Show that the magnetic field strength B<sub>R</sub> due to R at a point on Z is</p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"B_{R}=\\frac{\\mu _{0}}{2\\pi}\\frac{I_{R}}{r}\"></span></p>\n<p>where r is the distance between R and Z.</p><p>[2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b.ii\"><p><strong>b.ii</strong> Derive the units of <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\mu _{0}\"></span> in SI fundamental units. [1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c\"><p><strong>c</strong> </p><p>A small coil of wire of radius 2.0 cm and 20 turns is now located with its centre 10.0 cm from R. The current in R is kept constant at 2.0 A.</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_03\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 3\" src=\"asset:fig_03\"/></figure></p><p></p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c.i\"><p><strong>c.i</strong> Explain why there is no current induced in the coil. [2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c.ii\"><p><strong>c.ii</strong> </p><p>The current in R increases at a constant rate from 2.0 A to 10.0 A in 0.5 seconds.</p>\n<p>Calculate the emf induced in the coil.</p><p>[3]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c.iii\"><p><strong>c.iii</strong> Deduce the direction of the current induced in (c)(ii). [2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"d\"><p><strong>d</strong> </p><p>The coil is made to rotate such that an emf is induced. The graph shows the variation with time of the emf induced in the coil.</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_04\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 4\" src=\"asset:fig_04\"/></figure></p>\n<p>The frequency of rotation is doubled. Draw on the graph the variation with time of the new emf induced.</p><p>[2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"e\"><p><strong>e</strong> An electron is moving at constant velocity v = 9.0 × 10<sup>5</sup> m s<sup>−1</sup> parallel to a rod T, at a perpendicular distance d. T is connected to a cell via a switch. When the switch is closed the electron’s path curves with initial radius r. The magnetic field strength at the position of the electron at d is 4.0 × 10<sup>−6 </sup>T.</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"e.i\"><p><strong>e.i</strong> Calculate the initial radius of the electron’s path. [3]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"e.ii\"><p><strong>e.ii</strong> Explain the subsequent change in the radius of the electron’s path. [2]</p></section>"
  },
